ages 234-250. Springer-Verlag, 2005. Compact Representations over the Ring TL_{N}\nL281: Let N = pq be the product of two large primes and Z_{N} denote the ring of integers modulo N. The isomorphism Z_{N} ≡ Ψ_{p} x Ψ_{q} induces an isomorphism between\nL282: \nL283: T_{r}(Z_{N}) anύ T_{r}(ψ_{p}) x T_{r}(ψ_{q}).\nL284: Current knowledge in cryptanalytic techniques implies that the hardness of factoring an RSA modulus N or computing discrete logarithms in a finite field of the size of N is broadly the same. Assuming that p and q are of equal size, the discrete logarithm problem in T_{r}(ψ_{p}) and in T_{r}(ψ_{q}) will thus not be easier than factoring N provided that r ≥ 2. For efficiency reasons, a smaller value of r yields better performance. Henceforth, the description will focus on T_{r}(Z_{N}) with r = 2.\nL285: Consider the Pell equation over Z_{N},\nL286: Cf_{2} -. x^{2} - Ay^{2} = ! where Δε Z_{N} is a non-square modulo p and modulo q. By Chinese remaindering, the set of points (x,y) ε Z_{N} X Z_{N} satisfying this equation form a group, C(Z_{N}) = C(ψ_{p}) x C(ψ_{q}), under the 'chord-and-tangent' law (see \"Parametrization of T_{2}(ψ_{p})\"). The neutral element is O = (1,0). For each point T ε C(Z_{N}), there exists a unique pair of points T_{p} ε C(ψ_{p}) and 3^{>} _{q} ε C(ψ_{q}) such that T mod p = T_{p} and T mod q = T_{q}.\nL287: This equivalence is denoted by T = [τ_{p},\nL288: It is now possible to extend the previous compression map (cf. Eq. (3)) to Z_{N}. The only complication is that they are some points of the form [JV 0_{q}] or [ø_{p},:P_{q}]. To deal more easily with these points, a projective representation is considered for the compressed result, fh is written as a pair (M : Z) and fn = (M: Z) is said to be equivalent to fn' = (M': Z') if there exists some t ε Z_{N} such that M' = tM and Z' = tZ. Definition: ip-^{1} : C(Z_{N}) → FKZ_{N})XX_{1} Y) ^ m = (Ay: x - 1) (6)\nL289: This in turn leads to the definition of T_{2} (Z_{N}),\nL290: T_{2} (Z_{N}) = {fn I fn = xp^{'}Kx.y) with (x,y) E C(Z_{N})] (J) Group law. The group law (torus multiplication) on T_{2} (Z_{N}) is denoted (x). The neutral element is (t : 0) for some t e zjj. The inverse of an element M = (M -. Z) is (-M-. Z). From Eq. (5), given M_{1} = (M_{1}] Z_{1}) and M_{2} = (M_{2}-. Z_{2}) in T_{2} (I_{N}), a simple calculation shows that (M_{1}- Z_{1}) (X) (M_{2}: Z_{2}) = (M_{1}M_{2} + AZ_{1}Z_{2} : M_{1}Z_{2} + M_{2}Z_{1}) (8)\nL291: A further advantage of the projective representation is that the group law is complete: it works for all inputs M_{11}M_{2} ε T_{2} (Z_{N}).\nL292: \nL293: Affine parametrization. The map given by Eq. (6) does not yield a compact representation for T_{2} (Z_{N}) since each element M then requires two elements of Z_{N} . A possible workaround is to ignore input points of the form [JV 0_{q}] or\nL294: J^{>} _{q}] and to restrict to subset C(Z_{N}) = {(χ,y) ε C(Z_{N}) \\ x - 1 ε Z_{N}} u {O}. A point T = (x,y) ε C(Z_{N}) corresponds to M = (J^-: l) if T ≠ O, and M = (1: 0) otherwise. Definition\nL295: \nL296: T_{2} (Z_{N}) = [M I M = xp-^{1} (x, y) with (x, y) E C(Z_{N})] (9)\nL297: \nL298: From the above observation, an element M in T_{2} (Z_{N}) can be represented by an element of Z_{N} plus one bit: (J^-: l) or (1: 0). Yet another possibility is to represent\nL299: M as an element of AHZ_{N}) U (OO). Namely, if T = (x,y) ε C(Z_{N}) then M =\nL300: \nL301: (J^: l) if T ≠ O, and M = ∞ otherwise. Both cases result in a compact representation for T_{2} (Z_{N}) .\nL302: \nL303: The group T_{2} (Z_{N}) consists of all the elements of T_{2} (Z_{N}) together with a number of elements of the form (M : Z) with gcd(Z,/V) = p or q (corresponding to points\nL304: ^{in c}(^{z}w))- The ^{'}chord-and-tangent' law on C(Z_{N}), whenever it is defined, coincides with the group law on C(Z_{N}) = C(ψ_{p}) x C(ψ_{q}). The same holds for T_{2} (Z_{N}) . In practice, for cryptographic applications, N is the product of two large primes. It is therefore extremely unlikely that operation (x) is not defined on T_{2} (Z_{N}) .\nL305: \nL306: Torus-based vs. trace-based compression\nL307: Similarly to the section \"Trace-based compression\", Lucas sequences can be defined over the ring Z_{N} by Chinese remaindering. Trace-based or equivalently Lucas-based compressions are well suited to exponentiation. For example, Smith and Lennon proposed an analogue to RSA using Lucas sequence {V_{k}(P, l)} over Z_{w} (see Peter J. Smith and Michael J.J. Lennon. LUC: A New Public Key System. In E.G.\nL308: Dougall, editor, 9^{th} International Conference on Information Security (IFIP/Sec '93), volume A-37 of IFIP Transactions, pages 103-117, North-Holland, 1993).\nL309: When more than a mere exponentiation is required, trace-based representations are not applicable. Indeed, let P_{1} = (X^y_{1}), P_{2} = (^Vz) ^{e} C(Z_{N}) .\nL310: \nL311: Computing P_{3} = P_{±}® P_{2} being given P_{1} and P_{2} is easy: P_{3} = (X_{1}X_{2} + Δy_{\\}y_{2}, χ_{\\}y_{2} +\nL312: \nL313: X_{2}Y_{1}). However, computing x_{3} = X_{1}X_{2} + Δy_{t}y_{2} being only given xi and X_{2} is not possible.\nL314: Even an enhanced trace-based representation (cf. \"Trace-based compression\") does not seem helpful when working over Z_{N} . Here is an example of such an enhanced compression for Blum integers N (i.e. N = pq with primes p, q ≡\nL315: \nL316: 3 (mod 4)). As before, for y ε TL_{N} , par(y) is defined as y mod 2 (i.e. par(y) = y mod 2). In addition, chr(y) is defined as: chr(y) = 0 if (£) = 1 and chr(y) = 1 otherwise, where\nL317: \nL318: (!) denotes the Jacobi symbol of y modulo N. Since p, q ≡ 3 (mod 4), (=±) = (=±) =\nL319: -1. It is therefore easily verified that a point P = (x,y) ε C(Z_{N}) is uniquely identified by the tuple (x, β, χ) where β = par(y) and χ = chr(y), that is, with one element of Z_{N} and two bits. Unfortunately, decompressing (x, β, χ) into P = (x,y) requires the knowledge of p and q, which are, in most settings, private values. Unlike the finite field case, enhanced trace-based representation over Z_{N} allowing to multiply compressed elements is unknown.\nL320: Only torus-based representation over Z_{N} is available in this case to get a compact representation.\nL321: Extensions and generalizations Because the problems of computing discrete logarithms and of factoring were assumed to be balanced for an RSA modulus N = pq, the focus was on the case T_{2} (Z_{N}) . But the same methodology extends to higher-dimensional tori. It also generalizes to more general moduli; for example, to RSA moduli made of three prime factors. This allows for different trade-offs between the two computational problems. Applications\nL322: The compression technique herein reduces the parameter size (typically by a factor of two). This in turn reduces the requirements for storage and transmission. It saves a significant amount in cryptographic methods where many group elements are evaluated. The inputs of those methods are at least two elements in T_{2} (I_{N}), say\nL323: \nL324: M_{1} and M_{2}, to result in a third element M_{3} = M_{1} (x) M_{2} satisfying\nL325: on a torus modulo N parametrized by Δ; the computations being performed on a processor 10 (in Figure 1 ) or a crypto-processor. Figure 1 illustrates how the processor 10 receives input and outputs a result.\nL326: A first example is a torus-based implementation of the basic EIGamal encryption scheme (see Taher EIGamal. A public key cryptosystem and a signature scheme based on discrete logarithms. IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, 31 (4):469-472, 1985). Let N = pq denote a RSA modulus. The public key consists of an element g ε T_{2} (I_{N}) and of y = g^{r} ε T_{2} (Z_{N}) for some integer x. The corresponding secret key is x.\nL327: To encrypt a message m viewed as an element in T_{2} (Z_{N}), the ciphertext (ci,C2) is computed in T_{2} (Z_{N}), where C_{1} = g^{r} and C_{2} = m (x) y^{r} . Then, using secret key x, plain message m can be recovered from (ci,C2) as c_{2} (x) (c_{1})^{~r}.\nL328: A second example is the ACJT group signature scheme. To simplify the presentation, the various security lengths (λi, A_{2}, /i, /2) and corresponding ranges {A, F) are omitted (for details, see \"A practical and provably secure coalition-resistant group signature scheme\" already mentioned herein).\nL329: Slight modifications need to be brought to the scheme. The original ACJT group signature scheme makes use of a strong RSA modulus, that is, N = pq with p = 2p'+1 and q = 2q'+1 for primes p', q'. Since G_{Pι 2} (resp. G^ _{2}) has order p+1 (resp. 9+1 ), a RSA modulus N = pq is chosen with p = 4p'-1 and q = 4q'-1 for primes p', q'. Note that doing so -1 is a non-square modulo p and modulo q (i.e., p, q ≡ 3 (mod 4), which yields faster arithmetic. The subgroup of squares in T_{2} (Z_{N}) is denoted by T_{2}.\nL330: Finally, T_{2} is set f _{2} = T_{2} n T_{2} (Z_{N}). Being a group signature scheme, the modified scheme consists of five algorithms. The notation from \"A practical and provably secure coalition-resistant group signature scheme\" will be used.\nL331: Setup Select two random primes p', q' ≡ 1 (mod 4) such that p = 4p'-1 and q = 4q'-1 are prime. Set the modulus N = pq. Choose random elements a, ao, g, h in T_{2}. Choose a random element x ε TL*, _{q}, and set y = g^{x} ε T_{2}.\nL332: \nL333: The group public key is y = (N,a,a_{o},y,g,h). The corresponding secret key (known only to the group manager) is S = (p',q',x).\nL334: \nL335: Join Each user U_{1} interactively constructs with the group manager a membership certificate [A, e] satisfying\nL336: = a^{Xι} (x) α_{0} in T_{2} for some prime e,. Parameter X_{1} is the private key of U_{1} (and is unknown to the group manager).\nL337: \nL338: Sign Generate a random value wand compute in T_{2}\nL339: \nL340: T\" _ /o> _{Λ}.w T\" _ _{n}w T\" _ _{n}e_{l} /o> I_{1}W\nL341: \nL342: Randomly choose values n, r_{2}, r_{3}, r_{4} and compute, 